<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.5.4: Factoring Trinomials Using the “ac” Method and Substitution</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01665</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.5.4: Factoring Trinomials Using the “ac” Method and Substitution</md:title>
    <md:uuid>afa14f4f-10b5-417e-9143-e54f50dfa242</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity </title>
<para>Let’s learn a new method that always works on any trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\).</para>

<note>
  <para> <emphasis>How to factor trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\) using the “\(ac\)” method:</emphasis></para>
  
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Factor any GCF. Don’t forget!</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Find the product \(ac\).</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis>Find two numbers, \(m\) and \(n\), that</para>
  <list>
    <item>multiply to \(ac\): \(m \cdot n = a \cdot c\).</item>
    <item>add to \(b\): \(m + n = b\).</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Split the middle term using \(m\) and \(n\).</para>
  <para> \(ax^2 + mx + nx + c\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Factor by grouping.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> Check by multiplying the factors.</para>
</note>

<para>Factor each trinomial using the “\(ac\)” method.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(16x^2 − 32x + 12\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(4(2x − 3)(2x − 1)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(6y^2 + y − 15\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\((3y + 5)(2y − 3)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(48z^3 − 102z^2 − 45z\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(3z(8z + 3)(2z − 5)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>There will be times when a trinomial does not seem to be in the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\).</para>
<para>In some of these cases, we can make a substitution that allows the trinomial to be written in the \(ax^2 + bx + c\) form. This is called factoring by substitution.</para>
<para>It is standard to use \(u\) for the substitution. The substitution for \(u\) might be a variable with an exponent, such as \(x^2\), or it might be a binomial, such as \((x − 3)\).</para>
<para>Factor each trinomial using the substitution method.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(y^4 − y^2 − 20\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\((y^2 + 4)(y^2 − 5)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((x−5)^2 + 6(x − 5) + 8\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\((x − 3)(x − 1)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(x^4 − 3x^2 − 28\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\((x^2 − 7)(x^2 + 4)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
